1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,278
That's a CTO's question in the hour after
something went wrong, and it has two

2
00:00:09,318 --> 00:00:14,404
halves. Most engineers can answer the first
half. The second half, what must nobody

3
00:00:14,444 --> 00:00:18,419
run, is where the damage comes
from. Hold on to that second half.

4
00:00:18,759 --> 00:00:23,885
Undo is where Git costs its users the most
time. The textbook records that on the

5
00:00:23,925 --> 00:00:29,242
first of October 2026, the highest-voted
question under Stack Overflow's git tag was

6
00:00:29,282 --> 00:00:34,472
"How do I undo the most recent local commits
in Git?", and six more undo questions

7
00:00:34,512 --> 00:00:37,916
were in the top 25. The cause
isn't that undo is hard.

8
00:00:37,956 --> 00:00:40,020
It's that Git has no single undo.

9
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:46,971
Welcome back to Git and GitHub Deep Mastery.
Pull up a chair: this is Part 3, and

10
00:00:47,011 --> 00:00:52,185
the module on undoing changes. The commit
IDs in these recordings equal the IDs in

11
00:00:52,225 --> 00:00:55,110
the book, because replays
use the fixed clock.

12
00:00:55,450 --> 00:01:00,606
Git has five commands for undoing work:
restore, reset, revert, clean and stash.

13
00:01:00,646 --> 00:01:05,997
Each is defined by the places it writes to.
So before any command, this video gives

14
00:01:06,037 --> 00:01:11,128
you the map: four places, and one question
that decides between two families of

15
00:01:11,168 --> 00:01:11,453
undo.

16
00:01:11,793 --> 00:01:16,317
Then you take the first command, git
restore, which you met in the working-tree

17
00:01:16,357 --> 00:01:20,823
chapter. Today you see every form of it,
against one file that has a different

18
00:01:20,863 --> 00:01:24,925
version in each place. That way the
direction of every copy is visible.

19
00:01:25,265 --> 00:01:28,397
Everything on screen is Git,
with one callout to GitHub.

20
00:01:30,237 --> 00:01:31,650
After this video you can:

21
00:01:31,990 --> 00:01:36,746
Name the four places an undo can act on,
and the one question that decides between

22
00:01:36,786 --> 00:01:37,975
rewriting and adding.

23
00:01:38,315 --> 00:01:42,465
Say for each form of git restore where
the content comes from and where it goes.

24
00:01:42,805 --> 00:01:47,206
Restore a path from an older commit into
the index, the working tree, or both.

25
00:01:47,546 --> 00:01:51,425
State which forms of git restore
destroy work that no object holds.

26
00:01:53,265 --> 00:01:58,179
The four places. Every undo command writes
to one or more of these, and to nothing

27
00:01:58,219 --> 00:01:58,481
else.

28
00:01:58,821 --> 00:02:03,683
Read the rows with me: your files on disk,
the proposed next commit, the one commit

29
00:02:03,723 --> 00:02:07,286
ID your branch name holds, and
the commits reachable from it.

30
00:02:07,626 --> 00:02:11,739
Git's own manual draws the same lines
between the three main commands.

31
00:02:11,779 --> 00:02:16,723
git restore "does not update your branch".
git reset is about "updating your branch,

32
00:02:16,763 --> 00:02:21,588
moving the tip". git revert is about "making
a new commit that reverts the changes

33
00:02:21,628 --> 00:02:22,953
made by other commits".

34
00:02:23,293 --> 00:02:26,765
The deciding question: is this
history private or shared?

35
00:02:26,805 --> 00:02:31,878
A commit, one saved snapshot of the project,
is shared when a ref that other people

36
00:02:31,918 --> 00:02:37,054
can fetch reaches it. Everything else is
private: it exists only in your repository.

37
00:02:37,094 --> 00:02:41,428
On screen, the label origin/main is
your record of the server's branch.

38
00:02:41,468 --> 00:02:43,893
A and B are shared. C and D are private.

39
00:02:44,233 --> 00:02:49,171
Private history may be rewritten. git
reset, git commit --amend and git rebase

40
00:02:49,211 --> 00:02:53,001
replace commits with new ones,
or drop them from the branch.

41
00:02:53,041 --> 00:02:57,086
Nobody else can notice, because
nobody else has the old commits.

42
00:02:57,426 --> 00:03:02,248
Shared history is corrected by adding commits.
git revert records the inverse change

43
00:03:02,288 --> 00:03:06,184
on top, and everyone's next pull
brings it in like any other commit.

44
00:03:06,524 --> 00:03:10,281
The textbook insists that the reason
is mechanical, not etiquette.

45
00:03:10,321 --> 00:03:13,906
A branch in a teammate's clone
is a ref that holds a commit ID.

46
00:03:13,946 --> 00:03:18,567
If you move your branch backwards, their
ref still names the commits you removed.

47
00:03:18,607 --> 00:03:23,400
The server rejects your plain push as a
non-fast-forward, because your new tip isn't

48
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:28,003
a descendant of its old one. If you force
the push, the next teammate who pushes

49
00:03:28,043 --> 00:03:32,663
from a clone that still has those commits
puts them straight back, or merges them

50
00:03:32,703 --> 00:03:33,987
with your replacements.

51
00:03:34,327 --> 00:03:38,608
So, the CTO's second half: on a
shared main, nobody rewrites.

52
00:03:38,648 --> 00:03:39,175
You add.

53
00:03:39,515 --> 00:03:44,015
So before any undo that touches commits,
check which side of the line you're on.

54
00:03:44,355 --> 00:03:48,672
The last two read remote-tracking branches,
which record the state of the last

55
00:03:48,712 --> 00:03:52,638
fetch. Without the git fetch in front,
they answer for an older moment.

56
00:03:52,678 --> 00:03:54,147
The last module showed why.

57
00:03:54,487 --> 00:03:59,834
git restore. In one sentence: it copies a
stored version of the named paths into the

58
00:03:59,874 --> 00:04:03,746
working tree, into the index,
or into both, and moves no ref.

59
00:04:04,086 --> 00:04:10,058
Two choices define every form of it. The
destination: the working tree by default,

60
00:04:10,098 --> 00:04:14,090
the index with --staged, both
with --staged --worktree.

61
00:04:14,130 --> 00:04:18,196
And the source, which you can
name with --source=<tree>.

62
00:04:18,236 --> 00:04:24,355
Without it, the source is the index when
only the working tree is written, and HEAD,

63
00:04:24,395 --> 00:04:28,021
your current commit, as
soon as --staged is given.

64
00:04:28,361 --> 00:04:33,119
Inside .git: a restore rewrites
files, or blob IDs in index entries.

65
00:04:33,159 --> 00:04:36,930
A blob is the object that
holds the bytes of one file.

66
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:41,658
No object is created, no ref moves,
and no reflog entry is written.

67
00:04:41,698 --> 00:04:47,585
The reflog is the local list of the values
a ref has had, so there's no record to go

68
00:04:47,625 --> 00:04:48,150
back to.

69
00:04:48,490 --> 00:04:53,718
That last sentence gives the risk labels.
Every form that writes the working tree is

70
00:04:53,758 --> 00:04:56,289
🔴 DANGEROUS. --staged alone is 🟡 CAUTION.

71
00:04:56,629 --> 00:05:02,233
Quick quiz. You staged a change by mistake,
and you want it out of the index with

72
00:05:02,273 --> 00:05:06,972
your edits untouched. A, plain git
restore. B, git restore --staged.

73
00:05:07,012 --> 00:05:07,808
Your answer?

74
00:05:12,648 --> 00:05:16,358
B. With --staged alone, the
destination is the index.

75
00:05:16,398 --> 00:05:18,552
A overwrites your file instead.

76
00:05:20,392 --> 00:05:25,064
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy
for git restore is a photocopier with two

77
00:05:25,104 --> 00:05:29,775
output trays. You pick the original and
the tray, and whatever lay in the tray is

78
00:05:29,815 --> 00:05:30,299
replaced.

79
00:05:30,639 --> 00:05:34,001
It breaks at the replaced
sheet. You don't get it back.

80
00:05:34,041 --> 00:05:39,135
A photocopier leaves the old sheet on the
floor. git restore leaves nothing, unless

81
00:05:39,175 --> 00:05:44,332
that content had been staged or committed
at some point, and so exists as an object.

82
00:05:44,672 --> 00:05:49,402
For the whole module, hold this second
model. Ask two questions, in this order.

83
00:05:49,442 --> 00:05:52,482
One: is the thing I want
to undo private or shared?

84
00:05:52,522 --> 00:05:57,132
That chooses between rewriting and adding.
Two: which of the four places must

85
00:05:57,172 --> 00:05:59,124
change? That chooses the command.

86
00:06:00,964 --> 00:06:05,930
Every arrow of git restore points left:
from a stored version toward your files.

87
00:06:05,970 --> 00:06:07,932
No arrow touches the label main.

88
00:06:08,272 --> 00:06:13,670
Watch the first arrow move. Start
with 0.70 in all three places.

89
00:06:14,010 --> 00:06:17,910
You edit the file to 0.80,
and add it to the index.

90
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:22,388
You edit again, to 0.90,
and run plain git restore.

91
00:06:22,428 --> 00:06:27,386
The index is copied over the
file, and 0.90 is gone for good.

92
00:06:29,226 --> 00:06:35,471
0.70 in HEAD, 0.80 staged,
0.90 on disk, and status MM.

93
00:06:35,511 --> 00:06:40,613
Two commits further back,
the value was 0.50.

94
00:06:40,953 --> 00:06:45,890
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper: draw
those three boxes with today's values in

95
00:06:45,930 --> 00:06:50,928
them. That's a three-tree strip. Before each
variant, fill in a new one: which value

96
00:06:50,968 --> 00:06:52,847
will each place hold afterwards?

97
00:06:55,187 --> 00:07:00,297
Variant 1: plain restore. Because this is
the first 🔴 command of the module, the

98
00:07:00,337 --> 00:07:04,610
five answers. What it changes: the
named files in the working tree.

99
00:07:04,650 --> 00:07:09,181
What it can destroy: unstaged edits
in the named paths, without asking.

100
00:07:09,221 --> 00:07:14,396
Preview: git diff -- <path>. Recovery:
nothing brings back content that was never

101
00:07:14,436 --> 00:07:19,417
staged. Appropriate: when you have looked
at that diff and want to discard it.

102
00:07:19,457 --> 00:07:22,379
Predict the three values,
out loud. I'll wait.

103
00:07:27,219 --> 00:07:33,672
The index was copied over the file. The
edit to 0.90 is destroyed, and the staged

104
00:07:33,712 --> 00:07:37,439
0.80 stays. Status: M in
the first column only.

105
00:07:37,779 --> 00:07:42,286
Variant 2: --staged. 🟡
CAUTION. Predict the strip.

106
00:07:47,126 --> 00:07:52,840
HEAD was copied over the index
entry, and the file was left alone.

107
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:56,414
This is "unstage". Index 0.70, file 0.90.

108
00:07:56,754 --> 00:08:00,871
git reset -- <path> is the older
spelling of the same operation.

109
00:08:01,211 --> 00:08:04,112
Identical result, and
reset adds a status line.

110
00:08:04,452 --> 00:08:10,434
Now ask: where is 0.80? It's no longer in
the index, and it's not on disk, because

111
00:08:10,474 --> 00:08:16,309
the file had moved on to 0.90. A version
that existed only in the index is now a

112
00:08:16,349 --> 00:08:20,862
blob that nothing names. The next
video shows how to find one.

113
00:08:21,202 --> 00:08:23,444
Variant 3: both, from HEAD.

114
00:08:23,784 --> 00:08:28,079
Both places are set to HEAD's
version, and the status is empty.

115
00:08:28,419 --> 00:08:34,785
Variant 4: --source. The content comes from
a commit, here HEAD~2, two commits back.

116
00:08:34,825 --> 00:08:39,284
Predict the three values and
the status. Say them out loud.

117
00:08:44,124 --> 00:08:51,063
Without --staged, only the file is written:
0.50 on disk, the index still holds

118
00:08:51,103 --> 00:08:53,713
0.80, and the status stays MM.

119
00:08:54,053 --> 00:08:57,830
Variant 5: --source
with both destinations.

120
00:08:58,170 --> 00:09:02,956
Both places receive the old version. And
the last command proves that HEAD didn't

121
00:09:02,996 --> 00:09:07,843
move: it's still 9d940ca. This is an old
version of one file, placed on top of the

122
00:09:07,883 --> 00:09:11,060
current commit, ready to be
committed as a new change.

123
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:13,954
The older spelling. You'll
read it in scripts.

124
00:09:14,294 --> 00:09:19,435
git checkout <commit> -- <path> does what
the last variant did, index included.

125
00:09:19,475 --> 00:09:24,943
Likewise, older scripts write git checkout
-- <path> for git restore <path>, and git

126
00:09:24,983 --> 00:09:28,222
reset HEAD <path> for git
restore --staged <path>.

127
00:09:28,562 --> 00:09:34,114
Part of a file. git restore -p walks through
the differences hunk by hunk, and asks

128
00:09:34,154 --> 00:09:37,684
about each one. A hunk is
one block of changed lines.

129
00:09:37,724 --> 00:09:42,265
Here score.py has two unstaged
changes, a debug line and a real fix.

130
00:09:42,305 --> 00:09:47,722
The demo pipes the answers y and n in, so
each prompt is followed directly by the

131
00:09:47,762 --> 00:09:48,530
next output.

132
00:09:56,870 --> 00:10:01,579
Only the fix is left in the diff.
The debug line was discarded.

133
00:10:01,619 --> 00:10:05,273
-p also combines with
--staged and with --source.

134
00:10:07,113 --> 00:10:08,761
Five mistakes to watch for.

135
00:10:09,101 --> 00:10:14,165
Rewriting a shared branch to remove a bad
commit. Root cause: teammates' refs still

136
00:10:14,205 --> 00:10:19,269
name the removed commits, so a plain push
is rejected and a forced one is undone or

137
00:10:19,309 --> 00:10:21,544
merged by the next person who pushes.

138
00:10:21,884 --> 00:10:26,953
Checking "is it pushed?" without fetching
first. Root cause: @{u} and git branch -r

139
00:10:26,993 --> 00:10:31,262
--contains read remote-tracking
branches, which record the last fetch.

140
00:10:31,602 --> 00:10:35,982
Running git restore <path> to "unstage".
Root cause: without --staged the

141
00:10:36,022 --> 00:10:40,947
destination is the working tree and the
source is the index, so the unstaged edits

142
00:10:40,987 --> 00:10:43,550
are destroyed and the
staged version stays.

143
00:10:43,890 --> 00:10:49,439
Expecting git restore --source=<commit>
<path> to leave a clean, staged change.

144
00:10:49,479 --> 00:10:55,312
Root cause: without --staged only the file
is written; the index keeps what it had.

145
00:10:55,652 --> 00:10:59,089
Looking in the reflog for
the state before a restore.

146
00:10:59,129 --> 00:11:03,157
Root cause: a restore moves no
ref and writes no reflog entry.

147
00:11:04,997 --> 00:11:10,292
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation regressed
after someone tuned prompts/system.txt

148
00:11:10,332 --> 00:11:14,342
three commits ago, and those
commits also contain good changes.

149
00:11:14,382 --> 00:11:18,134
Reverting the commits would
throw the good changes out too.

150
00:11:18,474 --> 00:11:23,920
git restore --source=<good commit>
prompts/system.txt, followed by an ordinary

151
00:11:23,960 --> 00:11:27,647
commit, puts one file back
and leaves history intact.

152
00:11:27,687 --> 00:11:31,867
It's safe on shared branches,
because it only adds a commit.

153
00:11:31,907 --> 00:11:35,946
That's the map applied. The
history is shared, so you add.

154
00:11:35,986 --> 00:11:41,010
The place that must change is one
path, so you restore it from a source.

155
00:11:41,350 --> 00:11:46,564
And the platform side of the deciding
question. According to GitHub's documentation,

156
00:11:46,604 --> 00:11:51,818
a new ruleset, GitHub's named list of branch
rules, has "Block force pushes" enabled

157
00:11:51,858 --> 00:11:56,822
by default, and classic branch protection
rules disable force pushes by default.

158
00:11:56,862 --> 00:12:01,951
On a protected main, rewriting is normally
not even available, and a revert commit

159
00:12:01,991 --> 00:12:03,077
is the only route.

160
00:12:04,917 --> 00:12:11,525
Your turn. Do Lab 8.4, "Restore
variants", in lab-manual/m08-undo.md.

161
00:12:11,865 --> 00:12:16,439
Before each command of the lab, draw the
three-tree strip, and write the value you

162
00:12:16,479 --> 00:12:19,759
expect in each box afterwards,
plus the two status letters.

163
00:12:19,799 --> 00:12:23,529
Compare with the output only after
you've written all three values.

164
00:12:23,869 --> 00:12:28,038
One more prediction for the lab's failure
scenario: which of the versions you

165
00:12:28,078 --> 00:12:31,264
destroy could still exist as
an object, and which couldn't?

166
00:12:31,604 --> 00:12:37,555
The challenge is Exercise 8.7, Level
3, "the commit that came back", in

167
00:12:37,595 --> 00:12:40,339
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

168
00:12:42,179 --> 00:12:47,458
Q170: "A colleague asks for "the undo
command". Name the five commands that Git

169
00:12:47,498 --> 00:12:52,641
offers for undoing work, and say which
of working tree, index, branch ref and

170
00:12:52,681 --> 00:12:54,324
history each one writes."

171
00:12:56,664 --> 00:13:01,785
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
refuses the premise politely in one sentence,

172
00:13:01,825 --> 00:13:05,656
and then builds a grid: five
commands down, four places across.

173
00:13:05,696 --> 00:13:09,403
It fills the grid from the
definitions, not from typical use.

174
00:13:09,443 --> 00:13:14,441
Then it adds the one question that has to
be asked before choosing a row, and says

175
00:13:14,481 --> 00:13:19,234
which commands are on which side of it.
If you can draw that grid in under two

176
00:13:19,274 --> 00:13:21,384
minutes, you own this module's map.

177
00:13:23,224 --> 00:13:26,151
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

178
00:13:26,491 --> 00:13:31,516
An undo acts on four places: the working
tree, the index, the current branch ref,

179
00:13:31,556 --> 00:13:36,769
and history. Private history may be rewritten.
Shared history is corrected by adding

180
00:13:36,809 --> 00:13:41,646
a commit, and I fetch before I decide
which it is. git restore copies a stored

181
00:13:41,686 --> 00:13:45,336
version over files or index
entries, and never moves a ref.

182
00:13:45,676 --> 00:13:51,282
--staged reads HEAD and writes the index,
the default reads the index and writes the

183
00:13:51,322 --> 00:13:56,861
working tree, and --source changes what is
read. Every form that writes the working

184
00:13:56,901 --> 00:13:59,818
tree can destroy edits
that no object holds.

185
00:14:01,658 --> 00:14:05,765
Read sections 11.1 to 11.3 of Chapter 11.

186
00:14:06,105 --> 00:14:11,037
You now have the map: four places, one
question, and every form of git restore.

187
00:14:11,077 --> 00:14:16,260
Practise it with a paper strip. Next time:
git reset, the three modes, and why hard

188
00:14:16,300 --> 00:14:20,413
is dangerous. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

189
00:14:20,453 --> 00:14:21,923
See you in the next one.
